# React state: extract & unify

## 1. Split components by which state they need

Do not pile unrelated `useState` into one component. If a subtree does **not** read some of the parent’s state, extract it so that state stays next to the UI that uses it (single responsibility, looser coupling).

- Parent keeps only state its own render/handlers need
- Child owns state that only it uses
- Pass data/callbacks down; do not lift state “just in case”

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — header re-renders / is coupled to form state it never uses
const Page = () => {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");
  const [draft, setDraft] = useState("");
  return (
    <>
      <h1>Search</h1>
      <input value={query} onChange={(e) => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <textarea value={draft} onChange={(e) => setDraft(e.target.value)} />
    </>
  );
};

// ✅ GOOD — each segment owns only the state it depends on
const SearchField = () => {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");
  return <input value={query} onChange={(e) => setQuery(e.target.value)} />;
};

const NotesDraft = () => {
  const [draft, setDraft] = useState("");
  return <textarea value={draft} onChange={(e) => setDraft(e.target.value)} />;
};
```

## 2. Mutually exclusive flags → one status

If several booleans cannot be true together (or must stay in sync), they are **one** discriminant, not many `useState`s.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — impossible / inconsistent combinations
const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(true);
const [isError, setIsError] = useState(false);
const [isSuccess, setIsSuccess] = useState(false);

// ✅ GOOD
const [status, setStatus] = useState<"pending" | "errored" | "completed">(
  "pending"
);
```

Prefer a union (or a small tagged object) over parallel flags like `isOpen` + `isClosing` + `didFail` when they describe the same process.

This includes overlays: a drawer and the dialogs opened from it cannot be open together, so they are **one** `openedModal`, not `isOpen` + `settingsOpen` + `installGuideOpen`.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — exclusive overlays as parallel booleans
const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);
const [settingsOpen, setSettingsOpen] = useState(false);
const [installGuideOpen, setInstallGuideOpen] = useState(false);

// ✅ GOOD
const [openedModal, setOpenedModal] = useState<
  "none" | "sidebar" | "install-guide" | "settings"
>("none");
```

Do **not** split those flags onto each button just to avoid rerenders — the parent still owns the discriminant because switching one overlay must close the others.
